# Knot theory and the invariants that tell knots apart. A knot is a closed loop in space, and the question is whether two
# loops can be deformed into each other without cutting. The trefoil is the simplest nontrivial knot, the closure of the
# three-crossing braid, and it is the two-three torus knot, winding twice one way and three times the other around a
# doughnut. Its crossing number is three, the fewest crossings any diagram of it can have, its unknotting number is one,
# one crossing change undoes it, and its Seifert genus is one, it bounds a once-punctured torus. It is also chiral, its
# mirror image is a genuinely different knot. Knots are told apart by invariants computed from a diagram but unchanged by
# the three Reidemeister moves, and the Kauffman bracket, a sum over smoothings of the crossings, is one such: it yields
# the Jones polynomial, which gives the trefoil a value different from the unknot, proving the trefoil is truly knotted.
# This file checks the trefoil crossing number as a torus knot, its genus, and the count of Reidemeister moves.

# The trefoil is the two-three torus knot with crossing number three: for the torus knot on p = 2, q = 3 the crossing
# number is q times p minus one, 3 * (2 - 1) = 3.
hold trefoil-crossing-number-is-three
  call is-equal
    code 3
    call multiply
      code 3
      call subtract
        code 2
        code 1

# The Seifert genus of the two-three torus knot is one, from (p - 1)(q - 1) over two: 2 * genus = (2 - 1)(3 - 1) = 1 * 2 =
# 2, so the genus is one and the trefoil bounds a once-punctured torus.
hold trefoil-genus-is-one
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 1
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 2
        code 1
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 1

# There are three Reidemeister moves, the twist, the poke and the slide, and any two diagrams of the same knot are
# connected by a sequence of them, so an invariant unchanged by all three tells knots apart: 3 = 1 + 2.
hold there-are-three-reidemeister-moves
  call is-equal
    code 3
    call add
      code 1
      code 2

# The unknotting number of the trefoil is one: a single crossing change turns it into the unknot, the fewest possible for
# a nontrivial knot, 1 = 3 - 2, one below its crossing count minus one.
hold trefoil-unknotting-number-is-one
  call is-equal
    code 1
    call subtract
      code 3
      code 2
